    "topicLabel": "General English · written",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "question": "Read the following passage carefully and write a precis in about one third of its length. Suggest a suitable title.(10) In the modern world, money is often seen as the ultimate measure of success and security. People devote their lives to earning more, believing that wealth guarantees happiness, comfort, and respect. While money undoubtedly plays a crucial role in meeting basic needs and providing opportunities, it is not the most important thing in life. Beyond a certain point, the pursuit of money can lead to stress, unhealthy competition, and strained relationships. True happiness and fulfillment often come from sources that money cannot buy—such as love, health, peace of mind, and meaningful human connections. A wealthy person without friends or health is not truly rich. Moreover, ethical values, compassion, and a sense of purpose add far more to a person’s life than material possessions. The world has seen countless examples of individuals who, despite limited means, lived content and impactful lives. Money should be viewed as a tool, not a goal. It is meant to support life, not define it. A balanced approach—where one earns enough for comfort but does not let money dictate all decisions— is essential for a wholesome life. As societies evolve, there is a growing realization that quality of life cannot be measured by income alone, but by the richness of experiences, relationships, and personal growth.",
    "explanation": "",
    "sourceReview": true,
    "sourceNote": "Written response and transcription awaiting source review.",
