    "difficulty": "medium",
    "question": "Write a précis of the following passage in about 1/3 of its original length, giving it suitable title.(10) Trees give shade for the benefit of others, and while they themselves must stand directly in the sun and endure the scorching heat of the day, they continue to produce the fruit by which others profit. They do not hoard their gifts, nor do they complain of the weather. The character of good and noble men is exactly like that of these trees. We must ask ourselves: what is the use of this perishable body, which is destined to decay, if no use of it is made for the lasting benefit of mankind? A life lived only for the self is a life wasted. Consider the natural world and the lessons it yields. Take sandalwood- the more it is rubbed against a hard surface, the more of its sweet scent does it yield to the air. Consider the sugarcane— the more it is peeled, crushed, and cut into small pieces, the more delicious juice does it produce for the thirsty. Look at gold—the more it is burnt in the crucible of the forge, the more brightly and purely does it shine. The men who are truly noble at heart do not lose these divine qualities even in the process of losing their very lives. To them, the external world is secondary. What does it matter whether common men praise them or not? What difference does it make whether great riches abide with them or whether they live in poverty? What does it signify whether they die at this very moment or whether their lives are prolonged for many years? Happen what may, those who tread in the right path of righteousness will not set foot in any other, for their integrity is absolute. Life itself is unprofitable and hollow to a man who does not live for the service of others. To live for the mere sake of living one’s own life is to live the base life of dogs and cows, driven only by instinct. Those who lay down their lives, even for the sake of a friend, or even for the sake of a complete stranger, will assuredly dwell forever in a world of eternal bliss.",
